WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.277
Now a different load: a pure twist.

00:00:02.277 --> 00:00:05.920
A torque T is applied to the wing box, and nothing else.

00:00:06.233 --> 00:00:14.648
In a single closed cell under pure torque, the shear flow is the same all the way around.

00:00:14.648 --> 00:00:15.593
Call it q.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.643
Each little piece of wall carries a force q d s.

00:00:20.643 --> 00:00:26.640
Its moment about the middle is that force times its lever arm.

00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:32.182
Lever arm times d s is twice the area of the thin triangle it sweeps.

00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:36.745
Going all the way around, the triangles fill the whole cell.

00:00:36.745 --> 00:00:40.853
So the torque is two times the enclosed area, times q.

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:51.225
For a five hundred by two fifty cell, A is one hundred twenty five thousand square millimeters.

00:00:51.225 --> 00:01:01.333
Under ten kilonewton meters, q is ten million over two hundred fifty thousand: forty newtons per millimeter.

00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:07.120
The torque also twists the box a little.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.953
The twist per unit length along the span is called the rate of twist, d theta by d z.

00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:22.673
How much it twists depends on the material's shear stiffness, the shear modulus G, and on how thin the walls are.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:32.480
The formula is: d theta by d z equals one over two A G, times the integral of q d s over t, all the way around.

00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:37.929
We'll call the integral of d s over t, for a wall, delta.

00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:48.526
For our cell, every wall is two millimeters thick, so delta around the cell is fifteen hundred over two: seven fifty.

00:01:49.666 --> 00:02:01.246
So the rate of twist is forty times seven fifty, over two times one twenty five thousand times twenty seven thousand.

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:12.826
That's four point four four times ten to the minus six radians per millimeter: about a quarter of a degree per meter.

00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:20.522
Real wings often have several cells side by side.

00:02:20.522 --> 00:02:26.713
With pure torque, there's no bending stress, so the booms don't matter at all.

00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:32.746
Each cell still carries its own constant shear flow: q one, q two, q three.

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:38.907
A wall shared by two cells has both flows on it, in opposite directions.

00:02:38.907 --> 00:02:41.273
So it carries the difference.

00:02:41.566 --> 00:02:48.726
The torque is the sum of two A q for every cell.

00:02:49.566 --> 00:02:56.215
But that's only one equation, and there's one unknown shear flow for every cell.

00:02:56.215 --> 00:02:58.126
We need more equations.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.567
The extra equations come from one simple fact.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:08.100
The whole wing box rotates as one piece, so every cell must twist at the same rate.

00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:19.633
For each cell, the rate of twist is the same formula as before: one over two A G, times the integral of q d s over t around that cell.

00:03:24.033 --> 00:03:24.851
Your turn.

00:03:24.851 --> 00:03:29.593
Pause here and do checkpoint five point one in your guide.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:44.193
Going around cell one, every wall carries q one, except the shared wall, which carries q one minus q two.

00:03:44.193 --> 00:03:46.152
Collect the terms.

00:03:46.152 --> 00:03:53.007
You get q one times delta one, minus q two times delta one two.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:57.876
Set cell one's rate equal to cell two's.

00:03:57.876 --> 00:04:04.247
Together with the torque equation, that's two equations for the two unknowns.

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:12.022
Sometimes the skins and the spars are made of different materials.

00:04:12.022 --> 00:04:17.799
Then G is different on each wall, and it has to move inside the integral.

00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:22.244
Here's the trick.

00:04:22.244 --> 00:04:23.658
Pick one reference G.

00:04:23.658 --> 00:04:28.170
Then scale each wall's thickness by its own G over the reference G.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.739
That's called the modulus weighted thickness, t star.

00:04:34.666 --> 00:04:37.631
Now use t star wherever you had t.

00:04:37.631 --> 00:04:41.643
Delta becomes the integral of d s over t star.

00:04:41.643 --> 00:04:44.346
Everything else stays the same.

00:04:47.866 --> 00:04:55.466
Your slides' three cell example, worked this way, gives seven point one, eight point nine, and four point two newtons per millimeter.
